  ✓ CONFIRMED: Cross-margin liquidation valuation uses manipulable Uniswap 
    Justification: Upon reviewing the liquidation flow in 
CrossMarginLiquidation.sol and the valuation logic in CrossMarginAccounts.sol 
and PriceAware.sol, I confirmed the oracle price manipulation vulnerability. 
calcLiquidationAmounts() invokes belowMaintenanceThreshold(), which calls 
loanInPeg(account, true) and holdingsInPeg(account, true); liquidate() then 
repeats both valuations with true before calculating repayments and maintainer 
fees. In PriceAware.getCurrentPriceInPeg(), forceCurBlock does not use the 
cached EMA: when the cache is recent it calls viewCurrentPriceInPeg(), which 
directly computes a Uniswap reserve quote, and when stale it calls 
getPriceFromAMM(), which also returns the instantaneous quote even if it updates
the EMA. Thus both liquidation eligibility and settlement amounts depend on 
manipulable reserves from the configured liquidation path. An attacker able to 
temporarily skew that pair can make solvent accounts appear liquidatable or 
distort debt, collateral, and fee calculations, then unwind the trade after 
liquidation. The documented EMA and update-window protections do not guard this 
forceCurBlock path, and no deviation or TWAP validation is present.
  ✓ CONFIRMED: Liquidation swaps execute against manipulable AMM reserves w
    Justification: Upon reviewing CrossMarginLiquidation.liquidateToPeg and 
liquidateFromPeg, PriceAware.sol, and MarginRouter.sol, I confirmed the 
liquidation execution price manipulation vulnerability. liquidateToPeg calls 
authorizedSwapExactT4T with amountOutMin set to zero, while liquidateFromPeg 
calls authorizedSwapT4ExactT with amountInMax set to uint256.max. MarginRouter 
computes execution amounts directly from current Uniswap-style reserves through 
getAmountsOut or getAmountsIn, and its subsequent balance check only verifies 
that the AMM delivered the already-quoted amount; it does not compare execution 
against an independent valuation. The cached EMA-style price in PriceAware is 
used for valuation and borrowing logic, but is not used to derive liquidation 
slippage limits. Consequently, an attacker can trade against the configured 
liquidation pair before the liquidation transaction, skewing reserves so that 
the protocol accepts a poor output when selling collateral or pays excessive peg
input when acquiring debt assets, then potentially reverse the trade afterward. 
noIntermediary and the EOA-oriented liquidation design do not prevent public 
mempool reserve manipulation.


